full_content:
I have a confession to make. When I first saw that Tether—the behemoth behind the $110 billion USDT stablecoin—was the primary financial backer of Holepunch, Keet, and the Bare JavaScript runtime, my brain short-circuited.
I struggled with the cognitive dissonance. Why? Because if you have ever written a smart contract that interacts with USDT on Ethereum Mainnet, you know it is an absolute nightmare.
Before we can talk about Tether’s brilliant vision for a decentralized, serverless future, we have to talk about the trauma they inflicted on a generation of Solidity developers.
1. The Original Sin: USDT is not actually an ERC-20
When you are deep in protocol-level engineering, you rely on standards. EIP-20 explicitly states that a token's transfer and transferFrom functions must return a boolean value to indicate success or failure.
// The standard EIP-20 Interface
interface IERC20 {
    function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool);
}
Tether completely ignored this. When they deployed the USDT contract, they omitted the return value entirely. Their functions return void.
// The actual USDT Mainnet Implementation (simplified)
function transfer(address to, uint value) public {
    // ... logic ...
    // Notice: No return statement.
}
If you blindly write a vault or a swap contract using the standard IERC20 interface to move USDT, your transaction will seamlessly execute the logic, move the funds, and then violently revert at the very last microsecond.
Why? Because modern Solidity uses a strict ABI decoder. When your contract calls USDT.transfer(), the EVM executes a low-level CALL. When the call finishes, Solidity checks the RETURNDATASIZE. Since it expects a bool (32 bytes), but USDT returns absolutely nothing (0 bytes), the decoder panics and reverts the entire transaction.
For years, the only way to build DeFi safely with USDT has been to wrap it in OpenZeppelin's SafeERC20 library, which uses low-level assembly to explicitly check the return data size and bypass the strict ABI decoding:
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/utils/SafeERC20.sol";
contract Vault {
    using SafeERC20 for IERC20;
    IERC20 public usdt;
    function deposit(uint256 amount) external {
        // We have to use safeTransferFrom because USDT is non-compliant
        usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
    }
}
So, imagine my skepticism. The company that deployed a non-compliant proxy contract that breaks standard EVM interfaces is now funding the most radical, bare-metal, peer-to-peer web infrastructure of the decade?
It felt like a bad joke. Until I looked at the system architecture.
2. The Geopolitics of Decentralized Routing
Tether isn't funding Holepunch out of the goodness of their hearts. They are doing it out of existential necessity.
Look at the architectural dependency graph of the modern financial system. Tether has achieved incredible product-market fit; USDT is the shadow dollar of the global south. But no matter how secure the Ethereum, Tron, or Solana blockchains are, the access layer to those blockchains is controlled by a tiny cartel of Web2 tech monopolies.
[ Traditional Web3 Access Flow ]
User Wallet (Browser) 
      │
      ▼
Cloud-Hosted React Frontend (AWS / Vercel) ──► DNS Providers (Cloudflare)
      │
      ▼
Centralized RPC Node (Infura / Alchemy)
      │
      ▼
The Blockchain (Decentralized)
If a sovereign state decides to sanction Tether, they don't have to attack the blockchain. They just force Amazon Web Services to drop the frontend hosting, force Cloudflare to revoke the DNS, and force Infura to block the RPC requests. The blockchain keeps ticking, but the users go dark.
This is the exact vulnerability Tether is actively engineering out of existence.
3. Holepunch: Bypassing the Cloud
By funding the Bare runtime and the Holepunch protocol, Tether is building an application delivery layer that completely bypasses centralized cloud infrastructure.
The Bitfinex Blog
Instead of hosting a trading terminal on AWS, Tether can build a financial application where the binary, the UI, and the data are seeded via Hyperdrive (a BitTorrent-like distributed file system).
When a user opens the application, they connect directly to other users via a Kademlia Distributed Hash Table (DHT). There is no DNS. There is no cloud server.
[ Holepunch P2P Access Flow ]
Peer A (User) ◄──────── (Noise IK Handshake via UDP) ────────► Peer B (User)
      │                                                           │
      └──► Local Application Logic & State (Seeded via DHT) ◄─────┘
This isn't a blockchain. There is no global consensus, no blocks, and no gas fees. It is simply raw, encrypted, peer-to-peer data replication. Tether is building this to ensure that even if every cloud provider on earth blacklists them, people can still download, run, and communicate through their financial terminals.
The Architect's Realization
I spent hours frustrated by Tether's sloppy smart contract engineering. But looking at their macro-architecture, I have to respect the pivot.
They realized that putting a stablecoin on a decentralized ledger is completely useless if the physical wires connecting the users are owned by three centralized corporations. They are funding a non-blockchain JavaScript runtime because they understand that true decentralization requires severing the dependency on the server entirely.
We spend our time optimizing Solidity gas down to the byte and debating EVM storage slots. But if we don't start thinking about how our applications are actually distributed, we are just building decentralized sandcastles inside a centralized walled garden.

Learn TypeScript From First Principles to Senior/Staff-Level Production Engineering
If you searched for how to learn TypeScript properly — not just the syntax, but the thinking behind it — this guide is built for that. Most TypeScript tutorials stop at "here's an interface, here's a generic." This one goes further: it's a single, exhaustive TypeScript tutorial and reference that walks through the type system, object-oriented programming, generics, async programming, design patterns, SOLID and DRY principles, error handling, testing, and the tooling that real production teams run in CI — the same TypeScript best practices used at top-tier engineering organizations.
Whether you're a beginner looking for a structured TypeScript for beginners path, or an experienced JavaScript developer making the jump to advanced TypeScript and system design, you can read this end to end or jump straight to the section you need using the linked table of contents below.
Table of Contents
- 1. Introduction — What Is TypeScript & Why It Exists
- 2. Installation, Setup & tsconfig.jsonDeep Dive
- 3. Variables & the Complete Type System
- 4. Functions — Every Form, Overloads, this, and Best Practices
- 5. Arrays & Tuples
- 6. Objects & Type Aliases
- 7. Interfaces
- 8. Enums & Literal Types
- 9. Union, Intersection & Discriminated Unions
- 10. Type Narrowing, Assertions & Type Guards
- 11. Classes & Object-Oriented Programming
- 12. Generics — Basic to Advanced
- 13. Modules, Namespaces & Project Structure
- 14. Asynchronous Programming — Event Loop to Production Patterns
- 15. Advanced/Utility Types & the Type-Level Programming Toolkit
- 16. Design Patterns in TypeScript
- 17. SOLID, DRY, KISS, YAGNI — Principles Applied With Real TS Code
- 18. Error Handling Strategies
- 19. Testing TypeScript
- 20. Tooling, Linting, Build Systems & CI/CD for Production TS
- 21. Performance, Compiler Internals & Scaling Large Codebases
- 22. Interview Cheat Sheet (Expanded)
- 23. One-Page Quick Revision Sheet
1. Introduction — What Is TypeScript & Why It Exists
1.1 Definition
TypeScript (TS) is an open-source language developed and maintained by Microsoft. It is a strict syntactical superset of JavaScript — every valid JavaScript program is also a valid TypeScript program — that adds an optional static structural type system on top of JS semantics. TypeScript never changes JavaScript's runtime behavior; it only adds a compile-time layer that is erased before the code runs.
Key mental model: TypeScript types are a development-time tool. At runtime, there is no TypeScript — only plain JavaScript. This has a critical implication: TypeScript can be lied to (e.g., via as, any, unsafe casts) and it cannot protect you against bad data arriving from the network, disk, or user input unless you validate it (see §18 on runtime validation).
1.2 Why TypeScript? (Beyond the Basics)
| Reason | Explanation | 
|---|---|
| Compile-time error detection | Type mismatches, typos in property names, wrong argument counts, and null/undefined misuse are caught before shipping. | 
| Self-documenting code | Function signatures and interfaces act as always-up-to-date documentation, unlike comments which rot. | 
| Refactoring safety | Renaming a property or changing a function signature immediately surfaces every call site that breaks, across a codebase of any size. | 
| IDE tooling / IntelliSense | Autocomplete, jump-to-definition, and inline docs work reliably because the editor understands your data shapes. | 
| Scales with team size | In large teams, types act as a contract between modules/services owned by different people, reducing "what shape does this object have?" questions. | 
| Gradual adoption | You can introduce TS file-by-file into an existing JS codebase ( allowJs,checkJs), making it low-risk to adopt incrementally. | 
| Ecosystem | Virtually every major library ships type definitions ( @types/*or bundled.d.tsfiles), giving you typed access to the entire npm ecosystem. | 
1.3 JavaScript vs TypeScript — Concrete Comparison
// JavaScript — no static checks, error only discovered at runtime (or never, silently)
let user = { name: "Asha", age: 25 };
user.age = "twenty five"; // no error, silently corrupts data
// TypeScript — the type annotation creates a compile-time contract
let typedUser: { name: string; age: number } = { name: "Asha", age: 25 };
typedUser.age = "twenty five"; // Incorrect — compile error:
// Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.
1.4 How TypeScript Works Internally (Compiler Pipeline)
Understanding the pipeline is what separates "I use TS" from "I understand TS":
- 
Parsing — Source .tstext is tokenized and parsed into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).
- Binding — The binder walks the AST and creates symbols (a "Symbol" is TypeScript's internal representation of a named entity — variable, function, class, etc.) and builds scopes.
- Type Checking — The type checker walks the AST, resolves types (via inference and annotations), and validates every expression against the type rules. This is where 95% of "the compiler complains" moments come from.
- 
Emit — Type annotations are stripped and the AST is transformed/down-leveled to the JavaScript target (e.g., ES2020, CommonJS/ESM modules) and written to .jsfiles. Optionally,.d.tsdeclaration files and source maps are emitted.
Because type erasure happens at step 4, types cannot be used for runtime logic (e.g., you cannot do if (x instanceof SomeInterface) — interfaces don't exist at runtime; only classes, which compile to real JS constructor functions, support instanceof).
1.5 The Learning & Production Roadmap
Practice discipline that senior engineers follow:
- Enable strict: truefrom day one of a project — retrofitting strict mode onto a large loose codebase is far more expensive than starting strict.
- Treat the type system as executable documentation: if a reviewer has to ask "what shape is this?", the types are incomplete.
- Never treat anyas a shortcut to "make the red squiggly go away" — it is a designed escape hatch to be used sparingly and explicitly, not a default.


  2. Installation, Setup & tsconfig.json Deep Dive
2.1 Prerequisites
- Install Node.js (LTS version) from https://nodejs.org — TypeScript's compiler ( - tsc) runs on Node.
- Install TypeScript: 

   npm install -g typescript      # global CLI (fine for learning)
   npm install -D typescript      # project-local (required for production — pins version per-repo)
   tsc -v                         # verify installation
Production best practice: always install TypeScript as a local devDependency, never rely on a global install. This guarantees every developer and every CI runner compiles with the exact same compiler version, avoiding "works on my machine" type-checking drift.
- Initialize configuration:
   npx tsc --init
This generates tsconfig.json, the single source of truth for how the compiler behaves.
2.2 Compiling & Running
tsc                 # compiles the whole project per tsconfig.json
tsc app.ts          # compiles a single file, ignoring tsconfig module resolution nuances
tsc -w               # watch mode — recompiles on save; use during local development
node dist/app.js     # run the emitted JavaScript
For iterative development, most production teams skip the manual tsc + node cycle and use ts-node, tsx, or a bundler (esbuild, swc, vite) for near-instant feedback, then use tsc --noEmit purely as a type-checking gate in CI (bundlers strip types without checking them — tsc is what catches errors).


  2.3 tsconfig.json — Every Option That Matters in Production
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* --- Type Checking (strictness) --- */
    "strict": true,                       // umbrella flag — turns on all strict flags below
    "noImplicitAny": true,                // (included in strict) disallow untyped `any` fallbacks
    "strictNullChecks": true,             // (included in strict) null/undefined are NOT part of every type
    "strictFunctionTypes": true,          // (included in strict) sound function parameter checking
    "strictBindCallApply": true,          // (included in strict) type-check .bind/.call/.apply
    "strictPropertyInitialization": true, // (included in strict) class fields must be initialized
    "noImplicitThis": true,               // (included in strict) error on `this` with implied `any`
    "alwaysStrict": true,                 // (included in strict) emit "use strict" in JS output
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,     // index signature access returns `T | undefined` (huge safety win, NOT in strict by default)
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,   // `foo?: string` means "absent or string", NOT "string | undefined"
    "noImplicitOverride": true,           // require explicit `override` keyword when overriding base methods
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,   // catch missing `break` in switch statements
    "noUnusedLocals": true,               // flag unused local variables
    "noUnusedParameters": true,           // flag unused function parameters
    /* --- Modules & Interop --- */
    "module": "NodeNext",                 // or "ESNext" for bundler-based frontends
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",       // matches Node's actual ESM/CJS resolution algorithm
    "esModuleInterop": true,              // allows `import express from "express"` for CJS packages
    "resolveJsonModule": true,            // allows `import data from "./data.json"`
    "isolatedModules": true,              // ensures every file can be transpiled independently (required by esbuild/swc/babel)
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,         // forces explicit `import type` for type-only imports (faster builds, no ambiguity)
    /* --- Emit --- */
    "target": "ES2022",                   // JS language level of the OUTPUT
    "outDir": "./dist",                   // compiled output folder
    "rootDir": "./src",                   // source folder (enforces output mirrors input structure)
    "declaration": true,                  // emit .d.ts files — REQUIRED if you publish a library
    "declarationMap": true,               // enables "go to definition" into your TS source from consumers
    "sourceMap": true,                    // enables debugger breakpoints in original .ts files
    "removeComments": false,              // keep comments (JSDoc) in emitted .d.ts for consumer IntelliSense
    /* --- Interop / Safety --- */
    "skipLibCheck": true,                 // skip type-checking .d.ts files of dependencies (big compile-speed win, safe in practice)
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true, // avoid cross-OS casing bugs (macOS/Windows are case-insensitive, Linux CI is not)
    "allowJs": false,                     // set true only during incremental JS→TS migration
    "checkJs": false,                     // set true alongside allowJs to type-check the JS files too
    /* --- Paths --- */
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": { "@/*": ["src/*"] }         // path aliases — must be mirrored in your bundler/test runner config
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "**/*.spec.ts"]
}
Senior-level tsconfig guidance:
- Never ship a production repo without strict: true. Anything less is an unfinished migration, not a design choice.
- 
noUncheckedIndexedAccessis one of the highest-value flags most teams forget — without it,arr[i]andobj[key]are typed as if they always succeed, which is false and a common source of runtimeundefinedbugs.
- For monorepos, use TypeScript Project References ("composite": true,"references": [{ "path": "../shared" }]) sotsc --buildonly recompiles changed packages — this is what makes large monorepos compile fast.
- Keep tsconfig.base.jsonat the repo root with shared strict settings, and let each packageextendit — this is the DRY principle applied to configuration.
3. Variables & the Complete Type System


  3.1 let, const, and (avoid) var
let score: number = 98.6;      // reassignable, block-scoped
const PI: number = 3.14159;    // NOT reassignable, block-scoped
var legacy = "avoid";          // function-scoped, hoisted, error-prone — do not use in modern TS
Production rule: default to const. Only use let when a binding is genuinely reassigned (loop counters, accumulator variables, values reassigned in conditional branches). var should never appear in a modern TypeScript codebase — enforce this with the ESLint rule no-var.
3.2 Primitive Types
| Type | Meaning | Example | 
|---|---|---|
| string | Text | let city: string = "Hyderabad"; | 
| number | All numbers (int & float — TS/JS do not distinguish) | let price: number = 98.6; | 
| boolean | true/false | let active: boolean = true; | 
| bigint | Arbitrary-precision integers | let id: bigint = 12345678901234567890n; | 
| symbol | Unique, immutable identifiers | let key: symbol = Symbol("id"); | 
| null | Intentional absence of a value | let data: null = null; | 
| undefined | Declared but not yet assigned | let value: undefined; | 


  3.3 The Special Types — any, unknown, void, never
// any — completely disables type checking for that value. AVOID.
let danger: any = "hello";
danger.toUpperCase().thisMethodDoesNotExist(); // compiles fine, crashes at runtime
// unknown — the type-safe counterpart to any. Anything can be assigned TO it,
// but it cannot be USED until narrowed. This is the correct type for
// "data I don't yet know the shape of" (e.g., API responses, JSON.parse results).
let result: unknown = fetchSomething();
if (typeof result === "string") {
  console.log(result.toUpperCase()); // Correct — safe — narrowed
}
// void — a function that returns nothing meaningful.
function logMessage(msg: string): void {
  console.log(msg);
}
// never — a value that can never occur: functions that always throw,
// or infinite loops, or the empty branch of an exhaustive union.
function fail(message: string): never {
  throw new Error(message);
}
any vs unknown — the single most important type-safety decision in TS. any opts a value entirely out of the type system (bidirectionally assignable to and from anything). unknown opts in to safety — it can receive anything, but you must prove its shape (via typeof, instanceof, a type guard, or a schema validator) before you can operate on it. Rule for production code: ban any via ESLint (@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any), and use unknown at every true boundary (API responses, JSON.parse, third-party untyped libraries, catch clause errors).
3.4 Type Inference vs Explicit Annotation
let inferred = "hello";       // TS infers `string` — no annotation needed
let explicit: string = "hi";  // explicit annotation — useful for function signatures & public APIs
Guideline: let TypeScript infer types for local variables (let x = 5), but always explicitly annotate function parameters and exported/public function return types. Inference on locals reduces noise; explicit annotations on public boundaries prevent "inference drift," where a well-intentioned internal refactor silently changes a public contract.


  3.5 null vs undefined in Strict Mode
With strictNullChecks (part of strict: true), null and undefined are not automatically part of every type — they must be explicitly included:
let name: string;
name = null;      // Incorrect — `null` is not assignable to `string`
let maybeName: string | null = null; // Correct — explicit opt-in
This single flag eliminates the majority of "cannot read property of undefined" runtime crashes that plague plain JavaScript, by forcing every possibly-absent value to be handled at the type level.


  4. Functions — Every Form, Overloads, this, and Best Practices
4.1 Function Declaration Syntax
function functionName(param1: Type1, param2: Type2): ReturnType {
  // body
  return value;
}
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
4.2 Return Types
Always annotate the return type of exported functions. It (a) documents intent, (b) prevents the function body from accidentally widening the return type during a refactor, and (c) speeds up compilation because TS doesn't need to infer it.
function greet(name: string): string {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}
4.3 Optional & Default Parameters
function greetUser(name: string, age?: number): string {
  return age ? `Hello ${name}, age ${age}` : `Hello ${name}`;
}
function multiply(x: number, y: number = 1): number {
  return x * y;
}
multiply(5);     // 5  (y defaults to 1)
multiply(5, 3);  // 15
Rule: optional parameters (?) and parameters with defaults must always come after required parameters — TypeScript enforces this because JS call sites are positional.
4.4 Rest Parameters
function sumAll(...nums: number[]): number {
  return nums.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0);
}
sumAll(1, 2, 3);       // 6
sumAll(5, 10, 15, 20); // 50
4.5 Arrow Functions
const square = (x: number): number => x * x;
const add = (a: number, b: number): number => a + b;
const isAdult = (age: number): boolean => {
  return age >= 18;
};
Arrow functions do not bind their own this — they capture this lexically from the enclosing scope. This makes them the correct default choice for callbacks (event handlers, array methods, promise .then chains) where you want this to refer to the surrounding class instance rather than the caller.


  4.6 Typing this Explicitly
interface Button {
  label: string;
  onClick(this: Button, event: MouseEvent): void;
}
The this parameter is a compile-time-only fictitious first parameter — it's erased at emit and exists purely so the compiler can validate how the function is intended to be invoked.
4.7 Function Overloads
Overloads let a single function have multiple valid call signatures with return types that depend on the input — something a plain union parameter type cannot express precisely.
function parseInput(input: string): string[];
function parseInput(input: number): number[];
function parseInput(input: string | number): string[] | number[] {
  if (typeof input === "string") return input.split("");
  return [input];
}
const a = parseInput("abc"); // typed as string[]
const b = parseInput(123);   // typed as number[]
4.8 Function Types as Values
type MathOperation = (a: number, b: number) => number;
const subtract: MathOperation = (a, b) => a - b;
function applyOperation(a: number, b: number, op: MathOperation): number {
  return op(a, b);
}
Defining a type alias for a callback signature (rather than inlining it everywhere it's used) is a direct application of DRY — the shape is defined once and reused across every consumer.
4.9 Production Best Practices for Functions
- Keep functions small and single-purpose (this is the Single Responsibility Principle applied at the function level — see §17).
- Prefer pure functions (output depends only on input, no side effects) wherever business logic doesn't strictly require I/O — they are trivially testable and safely composable.
- Avoid boolean parameter flags that change behavior (function save(data, force: boolean)) — they force the reader to look up the function definition to understand a call site. Prefer an options object:function save(data, options: { force: boolean }).
- Use rest parameters instead of arguments(which is untyped and excluded from arrow functions entirely).
- Never mutate parameters; treat all function inputs as read-only unless the function's entire purpose is to mutate (and even then, prefer explicit "Builder"/"Mutator" naming to signal it).
5. Arrays & Tuples
5.1 Arrays
let numbers: number[] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let names: Array<string> = ["Asha", "Ravi", "Kiran"]; // equivalent generic syntax
let flags: boolean[] = [true, false, true];
console.log(numbers[0]); // 1
names.push("Meera");
numbers.pop();
T[] and Array<T> are 100% equivalent — the generic form is preferred when T itself is a complex union, e.g., Array<string | number> reads more clearly than (string | number)[] in some style guides, but both are correct.
5.2 Readonly Arrays
const readonlyNumbers: ReadonlyArray<number> = [10, 20, 30];
// equivalently: const readonlyNumbers: readonly number[] = [10, 20, 30];
readonlyNumbers[0];        // Correct — 10
readonlyNumbers.push(40);  // Incorrect — push does not exist on ReadonlyArray
Production guidance: default function parameters that receive arrays to readonly T[] unless the function's job is specifically to mutate the caller's array. This prevents an entire category of "function silently mutated my array" bugs and clearly communicates intent at the type level — this is a direct application of the principle of least privilege / immutability-by-default.
5.3 Tuples
Tuples are fixed-length, fixed-order arrays where each position has its own type — ideal for representing small, structurally-fixed records like coordinate pairs or [key, value] entries.
let user: [number, string, boolean] = [1, "Mahesh", true];
console.log(user[0]); // 1  (number)
console.log(user[1]); // "Mahesh" (string)
5.4 Named Tuple Members (modern TS)
type HttpResponseTuple = [status: number, body: string, headers: Record<string, string>];
Naming tuple elements doesn't change runtime behavior but massively improves editor tooltips and readability at call sites.
5.5 Optional & Variadic (Rest) Tuple Elements
type Point2D3D = [x: number, y: number, z?: number];          // optional element
type StringsThenNumbers = [string, ...number[]];              // rest element
type ConcatResult<A extends unknown[], B extends unknown[]> = [...A, ...B]; // spread in a generic
5.6 Nested Arrays (Matrices)
let matrix: number[][] = [
  [1, 2, 3],
  [4, 5, 6],
  [7, 8, 9],
];
console.log(matrix[1][2]); // 6
5.7 Practical Patterns
// Sum
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const sum = nums.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
// Filter + map (prefer functional array methods over manual loops for readability & immutability)
const evens = nums.filter(n => n % 2 === 0);
const doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);
// Destructuring a tuple
const student: [number, string, number] = [101, "Mahesh", 95];
const [id, name, marks] = student;
Best practice: prefer .map/.filter/.reduce over manual for loops for transformations — they are declarative, immutable by default (they return new arrays), and compose well. Reach for for...of only when you need early break/continue or async iteration.
6. Objects & Type Aliases
6.1 Object Types
let user: { id: number; name: string; email: string; isActive: boolean } = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Mahesh",
  email: "mahesh@example.com",
  isActive: true,
};
6.2 Nested Objects
let employee: {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  address: { street: string; city: string; pin: number };
} = {
  id: 101,
  name: "Sita",
  address: { street: "Road No. 12", city: "Hyderabad", pin: 500081 },
};
For anything beyond a trivial one-off shape, extract nested object shapes into their own named type/interface rather than inlining — this avoids duplication if the nested shape is reused, and produces far more readable compiler error messages.
6.3 Optional Properties
type Product = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  discount?: number; // may or may not be present
};
6.4 Readonly Properties
type User = {
  readonly id: number;
  name: string;
  readonly email: string;
};
const user: User = { id: 1, name: "Mahesh", email: "mahesh@example.com" };
user.name = "Samsani";  // Correct — allowed
user.id = 2;             // Incorrect — id is readonly
readonly is a compile-time-only guarantee — it does not freeze the object at runtime (use Object.freeze() for that). It prevents accidental reassignment in your own codebase but does not stop a consumer from mutating the object via as casting or plain JS.


  6.5 The type Keyword (Type Aliases)
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  isActive: boolean;
};
const u1: User = { id: 1, name: "Mahesh", email: "mahesh@example.com", isActive: true };
const users: User[] = [u1];
Type aliases centralize a shape definition so it can be reused everywhere (DRY), and — critically — when the shape changes, every usage site is re-validated by the compiler automatically.
6.6 Index Signatures
For objects whose keys aren't known ahead of time (e.g., a dictionary/map):
type ScoreBoard = {
  [playerName: string]: number;
};
const scores: ScoreBoard = { alice: 95, bob: 88 };
With noUncheckedIndexedAccess enabled (recommended — see §2.3), scores["carol"] is correctly typed as number | undefined, forcing you to handle the "key might not exist" case rather than assuming it's always a number.


  6.7 satisfies — Validate Without Widening (TS 4.9+)
A common production pain point: annotating a literal with a type loses the literal's specific inferred type (widened to the annotation), while not annotating loses validation. satisfies gives you both:
type RGB = [number, number, number];
const palette = {
  red: [255, 0, 0],
  green: "#00ff00", // Incorrect — TS error caught immediately, even though palette isn't annotated as Record<string, RGB>
} satisfies Record<string, RGB | string>;
// palette.red is still inferred as [number, number, number], not widened to RGB | string
satisfies is now the idiomatic way to validate configuration objects, route tables, and literal maps without sacrificing precise inference — prefer it over a plain type annotation whenever you want both validation and literal inference.
7. Interfaces
7.1 Interface Basics
Interfaces define the structure/contract of an object. TypeScript uses structural typing ("duck typing"): any object matching the shape satisfies the interface, regardless of how it was declared.
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  isActive: boolean;
}
let user: User = { id: 1, name: "Mahesh", email: "mahesh@example.com", isActive: true };
7.2 Extending Interfaces
interface Person {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}
interface Employee extends Person {
  department: string;
  salary: number;
}
let emp: Employee = { id: 101, name: "Sita", department: "QA", salary: 75000 };
An interface can extend multiple interfaces: interface A extends B, C { ... }.
7.3 Optional & Readonly Members
interface Product {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  discount?: number;     // optional
  description?: string;
}
interface Config {
  readonly apiUrl: string;
  readonly timeout: number;
  retries: number;
}
7.4 Interfaces for Function & Callable Shapes
interface Comparator<T> {
  (a: T, b: T): number; // call signature
}
const byAge: Comparator<{ age: number }> = (a, b) => a.age - b.age;
7.5 Declaration Merging (Unique to Interfaces)
interface Window {
  myCustomProperty: string;
}
// This second declaration MERGES with the first — both members exist on Window.
interface Window {
  anotherProperty: number;
}
Declaration merging is why interfaces are the standard choice for augmenting third-party or global types (e.g., extending Express's Request object with a custom user property in middleware). type aliases cannot do this — attempting to declare the same type alias twice is a compile error.
7.6 Interface vs Type Alias — The Real Decision Matrix
| Feature | interface | type | 
|---|---|---|
| Declaring object/class shapes | Yes — Idiomatic | Yes — Works | 
| Extending | extends(clear error messages) | &intersection (can produceneveron conflicts, harder to debug) | 
| Declaration merging | Yes — Supported | No — Not supported | 
| Unions, tuples, primitives, mapped/conditional types | No — Not supported | Yes — Only typecan do this | 
| Implements (by classes) | Yes — Natural fit | Yes — Also works | 
| Performance on huge object shapes | Generally faster to check (named, cached) | Can be slower for deeply nested unions | 
Senior-level rule of thumb: use interface for anything that describes the shape of an object or a class contract (especially public API surfaces, since they support declaration merging for extension by consumers). Use type for unions, tuples, function types, mapped/conditional types, and anything that is fundamentally not "an object with named properties." Many production style guides (e.g., large-scale Google/Microsoft-influenced codebases) mandate interface for object shapes and type for everything else — consistency matters more than which specific rule you pick.


  7.7 Interfaces and Classes — implements
interface Shape {
  area(): number;
  perimeter(): number;
}
class Rectangle implements Shape {
  constructor(private width: number, private height: number) {}
  area(): number { return this.width * this.height; }
  perimeter(): number { return 2 * (this.width + this.height); }
}
implements is a compile-time-only check that a class satisfies an interface's shape — it does not copy any implementation (unlike extends), and a class can implements many interfaces but only extends one class.
8. Enums & Literal Types
8.1 Numeric Enums
enum Direction {
  Up,      // 0
  Down,    // 1
  Left,    // 2
  Right,   // 3
}
let dir: Direction = Direction.Left;
console.log(dir); // 2
Numeric enums are bidirectional at runtime — Direction[2] returns "Left" — which is convenient for debugging but means the compiled JS includes a reverse-mapping object, adding bundle size.
8.2 String Enums
enum Status {
  Success = "SUCCESS",
  Failure = "FAILURE",
  Pending = "PENDING",
}
let s: Status = Status.Success;
console.log(s); // "SUCCESS"
String enums are not bidirectionally mapped, are more debuggable (console.log shows a meaningful string, not a number), and are generally preferred over numeric enums in production code for exactly this reason.


  8.3 const enum (Zero Runtime Cost)
const enum LogLevel { Debug, Info, Warn, Error }
const level = LogLevel.Warn; // inlined to `2` at compile time — no LogLevel object emitted at all
const enum is fully inlined and erased, producing zero runtime footprint — but it is incompatible with isolatedModules (required by esbuild/swc/Babel single-file transpilation) because those tools can't see across files to know what to inline. Most modern production toolchains therefore avoid const enum.


  8.4 The Modern Alternative: Union of String Literals + as const Object
Because of the caveats above, many senior TypeScript engineers now prefer this pattern over enum entirely:
const Status = {
  Success: "SUCCESS",
  Failure: "FAILURE",
  Pending: "PENDING",
} as const;
type Status = (typeof Status)[keyof typeof Status]; // "SUCCESS" | "FAILURE" | "PENDING"
function handle(status: Status) { /* ... */ }
handle(Status.Success); // Correct
handle("SUCCESS");      // Correct — plain string literals are also valid, unlike real enums
Why this is often preferred: it has zero runtime overhead beyond a plain object, is fully tree-shakeable, plays perfectly with isolatedModules, and — unlike TS enum — a plain string literal is directly assignable without importing the enum, which matters a lot when the value crosses a network boundary (e.g., JSON from an API) since enum values received as raw strings require an unsafe cast, whereas string literal unions accept them naturally.
8.5 Literal Types
let role: "admin" = "admin";
role = "admin";  // Correct
// role = "user"; // Incorrect — only the literal "admin" is allowed
8.6 Union of Literals
type DirectionType = "up" | "down" | "left" | "right";
let move: DirectionType = "up";
move = "left";  // Correct
// move = "jump"; // Incorrect — Error
Union literal types are the backbone of discriminated unions (§9.3) — one of the single most powerful patterns in production TypeScript for modeling state machines, API responses, and Redux-style actions.
9. Union, Intersection & Discriminated Unions


  9.1 Union Types (|)
A variable typed as a union can hold a value from any of the listed types.
let id: number | string;
id = 101;          // Correct
id = "QA-101";      // Correct
// id = true;        // Incorrect — Error


  9.2 Intersection Types (&)
Combines multiple types into one type that has all properties from all constituent types.
type Person = { name: string; age: number };
type Employee = { empId: number; department: string };
type EmployeeDetails = Person & Employee;
const e: EmployeeDetails = { name: "Sita", age: 30, empId: 101, department: "QA" };
9.3 Discriminated (Tagged) Unions — The Most Important Pattern in This Section
A discriminated union is a union of object types that share a common literal-typed property (the "discriminant" or "tag"). This is the idiomatic way to model states/variants safely in production TypeScript.
type SuccessResponse = { status: "success"; data: { id: number; name: string } };
type ErrorResponse = { status: "error"; message: string };
type LoadingResponse = { status: "loading" };
type ApiResponse = SuccessResponse | ErrorResponse | LoadingResponse;
function render(response: ApiResponse): string {
  switch (response.status) {
    case "success":
      return `Loaded: ${response.data.name}`; // TS knows `.data` exists here
    case "error":
      return `Error: ${response.message}`;     // TS knows `.message` exists here
    case "loading":
      return "Loading...";
    default:
      // Exhaustiveness check: if a new variant is added to ApiResponse and this
      // switch isn't updated, `response` here is NOT `never`, and TS raises a
      // compile error at this line — catching incomplete handling at build time.
      const _exhaustive: never = response;
      throw new Error(`Unhandled response: ${JSON.stringify(_exhaustive)}`);
  }
}
This exhaustiveness-check pattern (const _exhaustive: never = x) is a hallmark of senior TypeScript code. It converts "did the developer forget to handle a new case?" from a runtime bug discovered in production into a compile-time build failure — the type system enforces completeness for you.
9.4 Type Narrowing (Preview — full coverage in §10)
function printId(id: number | string) {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    console.log("String ID: " + id.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    console.log("Number ID: " + id.toFixed(2));
  }
}
9.5 Real-World Composition Example
type User = { id: number; name: string };
type WithTimestamps = { createdAt: Date; updatedAt: Date };
type WithRoles = { roles: string[]; isActive: boolean };
type FullUserRecord = User & WithTimestamps & WithRoles;
Guideline: use unions to express "one of several possible shapes" (variance), and intersections to express "compose several independent concerns into one shape" (composition). Reaching for intersection types to build up entities from small, single-purpose fragments is itself an application of composition over inheritance — see §17.
10. Type Narrowing, Assertions & Type Guards


  10.1 The as Keyword (Type Assertion)
let value: unknown = "Hello World";
let strLen: number = (value as string).length;
as tells the compiler "trust me, treat this value as type X" — it performs no runtime check or conversion whatsoever. Use it only when you have external knowledge the compiler cannot infer (e.g., you know a DOM query will return a specific element type). Misusing as to silence errors is functionally equivalent to using any and is a common source of production bugs — prefer type guards wherever possible.
const el = document.getElementById("app") as HTMLDivElement; // legitimate use
The double-assertion escape hatch value as unknown as TargetType exists for genuinely incompatible casts, but its presence in a PR should always prompt a reviewer to ask "why can't this be proven safely?"


  10.2 instanceof Operator
class Dog { bark() { console.log("Woof"); } }
class Cat { meow() { console.log("Meow"); } }
let pet: Dog | Cat = new Dog();
if (pet instanceof Dog) {
  pet.bark(); // Correct — narrowed to Dog
}
instanceof works because classes (unlike interfaces/types) compile to real JS constructor functions that exist at runtime.


  10.3 typeof Operator (Primitives)
function process(val: string | number) {
  if (typeof val === "string") {
    console.log(val.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    console.log(val.toFixed(2));
  }
}
typeof narrowing works for: "string", "number", "boolean", "symbol", "undefined", "function", "bigint", "object".


  10.4 in Operator (Property Existence)
type Admin = { name: string; role: string };
type RegularUser = { name: string; email: string };
function describe(person: Admin | RegularUser) {
  if ("role" in person) {
    console.log(person.role);   // narrowed to Admin
  } else {
    console.log(person.email);  // narrowed to RegularUser
  }
}
10.5 Custom Type Guards (User-Defined Type Predicates)
type Circle = { kind: "circle"; radius: number };
type Square = { kind: "square"; side: number };
type Shape = Circle | Square;
function isCircle(shape: Shape): shape is Circle {
  return shape.kind === "circle";
}
function getArea(shape: Shape): number {
  if (isCircle(shape)) {
    return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
  }
  return shape.side * shape.side;
}
The shape is Circle return type is a type predicate — it tells the compiler "if this function returns true, narrow the argument to Circle in the calling scope." Custom guards are essential once narrowing logic is reused across multiple call sites (DRY) or is too complex for a single typeof/in check.


  10.6 Assertion Functions (asserts)
A related but distinct pattern — instead of returning a boolean, an assertion function throws if the condition is false, and TypeScript narrows the type for all code after the call:
function assertIsString(val: unknown): asserts val is string {
  if (typeof val !== "string") {
    throw new Error("Expected a string");
  }
}
function process(input: unknown) {
  assertIsString(input);
  console.log(input.toUpperCase()); // narrowed to string here, no `if` needed
}
This pattern is common in production guard-clause / fail-fast validation at function entry points.


  10.7 Non-Null Assertion (!) — Use Sparingly
function getLength(s: string | null) {
  return s!.length; // "I promise this is not null" — no runtime check performed
}
! is one of the "Common Mistakes" flagged by senior reviewers when overused (see §22) — it silences the compiler exactly where it's trying to protect you from a real runtime crash. Prefer an explicit check (if (s === null) throw ...) or the nullish coalescing operator (s ?? defaultValue) instead.
10.8 Best-Practice Ordering of Narrowing Techniques
- 
Prefer discriminated unions + switch(compiler-enforced exhaustiveness) whenever you control the shape.
- 
Prefer built-in guards (typeof,instanceof,in,Array.isArray) for primitives/classes/known-shape checks.
- Write a custom type predicate when guard logic is reused or non-trivial.
- Use assertion functions at validation/guard-clause boundaries (start of a function, API handler).
- 
Reach for as/!only as a last resort, and always leave a comment explaining why the compiler can't prove it.
11. Classes & Object-Oriented Programming
11.1 Class Basics
class Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  constructor(name: string, age: number) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }
}
const p = new Person("Mahesh", 25);
11.2 Constructor Property Shorthand (Idiomatic TS)
Instead of declaring properties and then re-assigning them in the constructor body, TypeScript lets you declare and assign in one step by prefixing constructor parameters with an access modifier:
class Person {
  constructor(
    public name: string,
    private age: number,
    readonly id: number = Date.now(),
  ) {}
}
This is the standard idiom in production TS — verbose manual property declarations are considered a code smell once you know this shorthand.
11.3 Properties: Instance vs Static
class Counter {
  count: number = 0;              // instance property — each object gets its own copy
  static totalInstances = 0;      // static property — belongs to the class itself, shared
  constructor() {
    Counter.totalInstances++;
  }
}
11.4 Methods
class Person {
  constructor(private name: string) {}
  greet(): string {
    return `Hello, I am ${this.name}`;
  }
}
11.5 Access Modifiers
| Modifier | Accessible From | Notes | 
|---|---|---|
| public(default) | Everywhere | Implicit if omitted | 
| private | Only within the declaring class | TS compile-time only — see caveat below | 
| protected | The declaring class and its subclasses | Enables controlled extension points | 
| #trueprivate(JS native) | Only within the declaring class — enforced at runtime | True encapsulation, unlike private | 
Critical caveat: TypeScript's private/protected are erased at compile time — the emitted JavaScript has no actual access restriction, so reflection, JSON.stringify, or a runtime as any cast can still reach "private" members. For genuine runtime-enforced encapsulation (e.g., a security-sensitive value), use native ECMAScript private fields (#balance) instead:
class BankAccount {
  #balance: number = 0; // truly inaccessible outside this class, even at runtime
  deposit(amount: number) { this.#balance += amount; }
  get balance() { return this.#balance; }
}
11.6 Getters & Setters (Encapsulation)
class Temperature {
  #celsius = 0;
  get fahrenheit(): number { return this.#celsius * 9 / 5 + 32; }
  set fahrenheit(value: number) { this.#celsius = (value - 32) * 5 / 9; }
}
Getters/setters let you expose a clean property-like API while retaining the ability to validate, compute, or log on access — a key encapsulation technique.
11.7 Static Members
class MathUtil {
  static readonly PI: number = 3.14159;
  static area(radius: number): number {
    return MathUtil.PI * radius ** 2;
  }
}
console.log(MathUtil.area(5)); // 78.53975
Use static for utility/factory functions and constants that are conceptually tied to a class but don't need per-instance state. Overuse of static methods, however, is a common anti-pattern — it prevents dependency injection and mocking in tests (see §19), so reserve it for genuinely stateless pure-function helpers.
11.8 Abstract Classes
abstract class Shape {
  constructor(public readonly name: string) {}
  abstract area(): number; // must be implemented by subclasses — no body allowed here
  describe(): string {     // concrete method — shared by all subclasses
    return `${this.name} has area ${this.area().toFixed(2)}`;
  }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(private radius: number) { super("Circle"); }
  area(): number { return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2; }
}
// const s = new Shape("Generic"); // Incorrect — cannot instantiate an abstract class
Abstract classes are the right tool when subclasses share both a contract (methods that must exist) and concrete shared implementation (methods with a body). If there's no shared implementation, prefer a plain interface — it's more flexible (multiple implementation, no forced hierarchy).
11.9 Inheritance
class Animal {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  speak(): void { console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound`); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name: string, private breed: string) {
    super(name); // must call super() before using `this` in a derived constructor
  }
  override speak(): void { // `override` keyword (with noImplicitOverride) catches typos in method names being "overridden"
    console.log(`${this.name} barks`);
  }
}
const d = new Dog("Buddy", "Labrador");
d.speak(); // "Buddy barks"
11.10 Polymorphism
const animals: Animal[] = [new Animal("Generic"), new Dog("Buddy", "Labrador")];
animals.forEach(a => a.speak()); // each object's own `speak()` is invoked — runtime dispatch
11.11 Composition Over Inheritance (Senior-Level Guidance)
Deep inheritance hierarchies are notoriously fragile ("the fragile base class problem" — a change in a base class can silently break many descendants). Production-grade TypeScript strongly favors composing behavior from small, focused, injected collaborators over building tall class C extends B extends A chains:
// Incorrect — Inheritance-heavy — brittle, hard to test in isolation
class Logger {}
class AuditableService extends Logger {}
class UserService extends AuditableService {}
// Correct — Composition — flexible, each dependency independently testable/mockable
interface Logger { log(msg: string): void; }
interface AuditTrail { record(action: string): void; }
class UserService {
  constructor(
    private readonly logger: Logger,
    private readonly audit: AuditTrail,
  ) {}
  createUser(name: string) {
    this.logger.log(`Creating user ${name}`);
    this.audit.record(`create-user:${name}`);
  }
}
This is precisely the Dependency Inversion Principle (§17.5) and the standard pattern behind dependency-injection frameworks (NestJS, InversifyJS, Angular DI).
11.12 Interfaces + Classes: Design by Contract
interface Repository<T> {
  findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  save(entity: T): Promise<void>;
}
class InMemoryUserRepository implements Repository<User> {
  private store = new Map<string, User>();
  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> { return this.store.get(id) ?? null; }
  async save(user: User): Promise<void> { this.store.set(user.id, user); }
}
Programming against an interface (the Repository pattern, §16.6) rather than a concrete class allows the implementation (in-memory, SQL, or a mock for tests) to be swapped without touching consumer code — this is the core value of interfaces in OOP TypeScript.
12. Generics — Basic to Advanced
12.1 Why Generics?
// Incorrect — Without generics — duplicated logic per type (violates DRY)
function addNumbers(a: number, b: number): number { return a + b; }
function addStrings(a: string, b: string): string { return (a as any) + b; }
// Correct — With generics — one reusable, fully type-safe implementation
function identity<T>(value: T): T { return value; }
const n = identity<number>(42);     // T inferred/explicit as number
const s = identity("Hello");        // T inferred as string — explicit <string> not required
Generics let you write algorithms and data structures that are parameterized by type, preserving full type safety without duplicating code per type — the type-system equivalent of DRY.
12.2 Generic Functions
function firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}
12.3 Generic Interfaces
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  data: T;
  success: boolean;
  message?: string;
}
const userRes: ApiResponse<{ id: number; name: string }> = {
  data: { id: 1, name: "Mahesh" },
  success: true,
  message: "Fetched successfully",
};
12.4 Generic Classes
class Box<T> {
  private value: T;
  constructor(value: T) { this.value = value; }
  getValue(): T { return this.value; }
}
const numBox = new Box<number>(100);
const strBox = new Box<string>("TypeScript");


  12.5 Constraints (extends)
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(item: T): number {
  return item.length;
}
getLength("Hello");        // Correct — 5
getLength([1, 2, 3, 4]);   // Correct — 4
// getLength(123);          // Incorrect — number has no .length


  12.6 Multiple Type Parameters & keyof Constraints
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}
const user = { id: 1, name: "Mahesh" };
const name = getProperty(user, "name"); // inferred as string
// getProperty(user, "email");           // Incorrect — "email" is not a key of user
K extends keyof T is one of the highest-leverage generic patterns in production TS — it makes property-access helpers (getters, form-field accessors, ORM query builders) fully type-safe against typos.
12.7 Default Generic Parameters
interface PaginatedResult<T = unknown> {
  items: T[];
  page: number;
  totalPages: number;
}
12.8 Generic Constraints With Conditional Defaults
type ApiResult<T, E = Error> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: E };
12.9 Conditional Types
Conditional types let you branch a type based on a type-level condition, similar to a ternary but evaluated by the compiler:
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "yes" : "no";
type A = IsString<string>;  // "yes"
type B = IsString<number>;  // "no"
// Practical example: unwrap a Promise's inner type
type Awaited2<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T;
type Result = Awaited2<Promise<number>>; // number
The infer keyword introduces a new type variable to be inferred within the extends clause — this is how TypeScript's own built-in ReturnType<T>, Parameters<T>, and Awaited<T> utility types are implemented internally.
12.10 Mapped Types
Mapped types let you build a new object type by transforming every property of an existing type:
type Readonly2<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };
type Partial2<T>  = { [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };
type Nullable<T>  = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null };
// Key remapping with `as` (TS 4.1+)
type Getters<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};
interface Person { name: string; age: number; }
type PersonGetters = Getters<Person>;
// { getName: () => string; getAge: () => number }
12.11 Template Literal Types
Template literal types let you build string-literal unions programmatically at the type level — extremely valuable for typed routes, CSS-in-JS, i18n keys, and event names:
type HttpMethod = "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "DELETE";
type Endpoint = "/users" | "/orders";
type Route = `${HttpMethod} ${Endpoint}`;
// "GET /users" | "GET /orders" | "POST /users" | ... (8 combinations, generated automatically)
type EventName<T extends string> = `on${Capitalize<T>}`;
type ClickEvent = EventName<"click">; // "onClick"
12.12 Variance & Function Parameter Bivariance (Advanced Nuance)
TypeScript function types are checked contravariantly for parameters under strictFunctionTypes (part of strict). This matters when designing generic callback-accepting APIs — a function accepting a narrower parameter type is not automatically substitutable where a wider one is expected, which is the mathematically sound behavior and prevents an entire class of "safe-looking but unsound" callback bugs.
12.13 Production Guidance on Generics
- Name generic parameters meaningfully once there's more than one (T,K,Vfor trivial single-purpose generics;TInput,TOutput,TEntityfor domain-specific ones in larger codebases).
- Add constraints (extends) as narrowly as needed — an unconstrainedTin a function that clearly needs.lengthor.idis a missed opportunity for a better compiler error at the call site.
- Prefer generics over anyin every "any" generic-container situation — repositories, caches, event emitters, HTTP client wrappers, state containers.
- Don't over-genericize: if a function is only ever called with one concrete type in the entire codebase, a generic adds indirection without benefit — YAGNI applies to type parameters too.
13. Modules, Namespaces & Project Structure
13.1 Export / Import (Named)
// math.ts
export const PI = 3.14159;
export function add(a: number, b: number): number { return a + b; }
// app.ts
import { PI, add } from "./math";
console.log(add(5, 10)); // 15
13.2 Default Export / Import
// user.ts
export default class User {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  greet() { return `Hello, ${this.name}`; }
}
// app.ts
import User from "./user"; // any local name is valid — no curly braces
Production guidance: many senior-level style guides (and the TypeScript team itself, informally) now prefer named exports over default exports, because: (a) named exports are automatically refactor-safe (renaming the export renames every import via IDE tooling; default exports can be imported under any arbitrary name, defeating that), (b) named exports enable better tree-shaking analysis by bundlers, and (c) they support multiple exports per file naturally. Reserve default exports for framework-mandated conventions (e.g., a Next.js page file) where the tooling requires them.
13.3 Re-exports & Barrel Files
// utils/index.ts (a "barrel" file)
export { formatDate } from "./date";
export { slugify } from "./string";
export * from "./validation";
Barrel files simplify imports (import { formatDate, slugify } from "@/utils") but at scale can hurt build performance and tree-shaking (bundlers sometimes can't eliminate unused re-exports) — many large monorepos deliberately avoid deep barrel chains for this reason. Use them for small, stable, public-facing module boundaries; avoid them for large internal utility folders.
13.4 Type-Only Imports/Exports
import type { User } from "./types";  // guarantees this import is erased entirely at compile time
export type { ApiResponse } from "./api";
With verbatimModuleSyntax enabled (recommended, §2.3), you must be explicit about type-only imports. This matters because a plain import { User } where User is only a type could otherwise be ambiguous to non-type-checking transpilers (esbuild/swc), potentially leaving a dead runtime import in the bundle.
13.5 Namespaces (Legacy — Avoid in Modern Code)
namespace Geometry {
  export function areaOfCircle(r: number) { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}
Geometry.areaOfCircle(5);
Namespaces predate ES modules and are now considered legacy for application code — ES modules (import/export) are the standard in all modern TypeScript. Namespaces still appear in .d.ts files for very old third-party libraries and in some declaration-merging scenarios for global augmentation, but should not be used for new application code.
13.6 Module Resolution Strategies
- 
NodeNext/Node16— mirrors Node.js's actual ESM/CJS resolution rules (including the requirement for explicit.jsextensions in relative ESM imports even though the source files are.ts). This is the correct setting for Node.js backends.
- 
Bundler— for frontend apps using Vite/webpack/esbuild, where the bundler (not Node) resolves modules and extensions aren't required.
- Avoid moduleResolution: "Classic"— it's a legacy TS-only algorithm, not what any real JS runtime uses.
13.7 Recommended Production Folder Structure
src/
├── modules/                 # feature-oriented (preferred over layer-oriented at scale)
│   ├── users/
│   │   ├── user.controller.ts
│   │   ├── user.service.ts
│   │   ├── user.repository.ts
│   │   ├── user.types.ts
│   │   └── user.test.ts
│   └── orders/
│       └── ...
├── shared/
│   ├── types/                # cross-cutting shared types
│   ├── utils/
│   └── errors/
├── config/
│   └── env.ts                 # validated environment config (see §18.4)
└── index.ts                   # composition root / entry point
Feature-based ("vertical slice") organization — grouping by business domain rather than by technical layer (controllers/, services/, models/ folders scattered separately) — is the pattern most large production codebases converge on, because it keeps everything related to one feature co-located, minimizing cross-folder churn when a feature changes and making it trivial to delete a feature cleanly.
14. Asynchronous Programming — Event Loop to Production Patterns
14.1 The JavaScript Event Loop (Foundational Mental Model)
JavaScript is single-threaded. Asynchronous operations (I/O, timers, network) don't block that thread — they're handed off to the runtime (Node/browser APIs), and their callbacks are queued to run later:
- Call stack — currently executing synchronous code.
- 
Microtask queue — Promise .then/.catch/.finallycallbacks andqueueMicrotask. Drained completely before the next macrotask.
- 
Macrotask queue — setTimeout,setInterval, I/O callbacks,setImmediate(Node).
Understanding that microtasks always run before the next macrotask explains many "why did this log before that" surprises and is essential when reasoning about ordering in concurrent async code.
14.2 Promises
const promise = new Promise<string>((resolve, reject) => {
  const success = true;
  if (success) resolve("Operation successful!");
  else reject(new Error("Something went wrong"));
});
promise.then(data => console.log(data)).catch(err => console.error(err));
Always reject with an Error instance (or subclass), never a plain string — this preserves a stack trace and lets catch blocks reliably use instanceof Error.


  14.3 async/await
async function getData(): Promise<string> {
  return "Hello from async function";
}
getData().then(data => console.log(data));
An async function always returns a Promise, even if the body returns a plain value (it's implicitly wrapped) — this is a common early misconception worth internalizing.
async function loadMessage() {
  console.log("Fetching message...");
  const msg = await getData(); // pauses here until the Promise settles, without blocking the thread
  console.log(msg);
}
14.4 Error Handling in Async Code
async function getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    const user: unknown = await res.json();
    return validateUser(user); // see §18.4 — never trust `unknown` JSON without validation
  } catch (error) {
    // `error` in a catch clause is typed `unknown` under `strict`/`useUnknownInCatchVariables` —
    // you must narrow before using it.
    if (error instanceof Error) {
      console.error("Error:", error.message);
    }
    throw error; // re-throw unless this function is specifically meant to swallow it
  } finally {
    console.log("Request completed");
  }
}
14.5 Promise Combinators — Choosing the Right One
| Combinator | Behavior | Use When | 
|---|---|---|
| Promise.all | Waits for all; rejects immediately if any one rejects | All results are required; failure of one invalidates the whole batch | 
| Promise.allSettled | Waits for all; never rejects, returns per-item status | You need every result regardless of individual failures (e.g., batch notifications) | 
| Promise.race | Settles as soon as the first promise settles (resolve or reject) | Timeouts, "fastest wins" scenarios | 
| Promise.any | Settles as soon as the first one resolves; rejects only if all reject | Trying multiple redundant sources, first success wins | 
// Fetch multiple resources concurrently instead of sequentially — a very common perf win
const [users, orders] = await Promise.all([fetchUsers(), fetchOrders()]);
// Timeout pattern using Promise.race
function withTimeout<T>(promise: Promise<T>, ms: number): Promise<T> {
  const timeout = new Promise<never>((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("Timed out")), ms)
  );
  return Promise.race([promise, timeout]);
}


  14.6 Sequential vs Concurrent await — A Common Performance Bug
// Incorrect — Sequential — each await blocks the next, total time = sum of all requests
const user = await fetchUser(id);
const orders = await fetchOrders(id);
// Correct — Concurrent — both requests fire immediately, total time = the slower of the two
const [user2, orders2] = await Promise.all([fetchUser(id), fetchOrders(id)]);
Only await sequentially when a later call genuinely depends on an earlier call's result — otherwise, kick off independent async work concurrently.
14.7 Async Iteration
async function* paginate(url: string): AsyncGenerator<Item[]> {
  let next: string | null = url;
  while (next) {
    const res = await fetch(next);
    const page = await res.json();
    yield page.items;
    next = page.nextPageUrl;
  }
}
for await (const items of paginate("/api/items")) {
  process(items);
}
14.8 Fetch API Example With Typed Response
interface ApiUser { id: number; name: string; email: string; }
async function fetchUsers(): Promise<ApiUser[]> {
  try {
    const res = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users");
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${res.status}`);
    const users: unknown = await res.json();
    if (!Array.isArray(users)) throw new Error("Unexpected response shape");
    return users as ApiUser[]; // in production, validate with a schema library — see §18.4
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to fetch users:", (error as Error).message);
    throw error;
  }
}


  14.9 Cancellation With AbortController
async function fetchWithCancel(url: string, signal: AbortSignal) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
  return res.json();
}
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000); // cancel if it takes too long
fetchWithCancel("/api/slow", controller.signal);
Production async code — especially in UI code where a component may unmount, or backend code with client-disconnect handling — should propagate AbortSignal through call chains rather than letting orphaned requests run to completion wastefully.
14.10 Best Practices Summary
- Always handle errors with try/catcharound everyawait-ing async function boundary — an unhandled rejection crashes Node processes (unhandledRejection) and silently fails in browsers.
- Avoid "callback hell" — async/awaiton top of Promises is strictly preferred over raw.then()chains for anything beyond a single step.
- Never mix async/awaitwith.then()chains in the same function — pick one style per function for readability.
- Use Promise.all/allSettledto parallelize independent I/O — sequentialawaitis a top production performance anti-pattern.
- Keep async functions small and focused — a 200-line async functionhandling five different concerns is a Single-Responsibility-Principle violation just like any other oversized function.
15. Advanced/Utility Types & the Type-Level Programming Toolkit


  15.1 keyof
type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string };
type UserKeys = keyof User; // "id" | "name" | "email"
const key: UserKeys = "id"; // Correct


  15.2 typeof (Type Query, not the runtime operator)
const user = { id: 1, name: "Mahesh" };
type UserType = typeof user; // { id: number; name: string }
Combining typeof + keyof lets you derive types directly from runtime values/config objects — extremely useful for deriving a type from a single source of truth (DRY at the type level):
const routes = { home: "/", about: "/about", contact: "/contact" } as const;
type RouteKey = keyof typeof routes;    // "home" | "about" | "contact"
type RoutePath = (typeof routes)[RouteKey]; // "/" | "/about" | "/contact"
15.3 Built-In Utility Types — Complete Reference
type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string; age: number };
type PartialUser    = Partial<User>;              // all properties optional
type RequiredUser   = Required<PartialUser>;       // all properties mandatory
type ReadonlyUser   = Readonly<User>;              // all properties readonly
type Picked         = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;   // only id and name
type Omitted        = Omit<User, "email">;         // everything except email
type ScoreMap       = Record<string, number>;      // dictionary type: keys string, values number
type MaybeUser       = User | null;
type NonNull         = NonNullable<MaybeUser>;     // strips null/undefined -> User
// Record — dictionaries/maps
type ScoreMap = Record<string, number>;
const scores: ScoreMap = { math: 95, science: 88, english: 92 };
// Pick — select a subset
type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;
// Omit — remove specific keys
type UserWithoutEmail = Omit<User, "email">;
15.4 Function-Related Utility Types
function createUser(name: string, age: number): { id: number; name: string } {
  return { id: Date.now(), name };
}
type CreateUserParams = Parameters<typeof createUser>;   // [name: string, age: number]
type CreateUserReturn  = ReturnType<typeof createUser>;   // { id: number; name: string }
type CreateUserAsync   = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof getUserAsync>>; // unwraps a Promise return type
ReturnType/Parameters are heavily used to derive types from existing functions rather than redeclaring them — another DRY application, and crucial when wrapping/decorating third-party functions.
15.5 String Manipulation Utility Types
type Shout = Uppercase<"hello">;     // "HELLO"
type Whisper = Lowercase<"HELLO">;   // "hello"
type Title = Capitalize<"typescript">;   // "Typescript"
type Uncap = Uncapitalize<"TypeScript">; // "typeScript"


  15.6 Exclude / Extract
type AllRoles = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer" | "guest";
type PrivilegedRoles = Exclude<AllRoles, "viewer" | "guest">; // "admin" | "editor"
type ReadOnlyRoles    = Extract<AllRoles, "viewer" | "guest">; // "viewer" | "guest"
15.7 Deep Utility Types (Custom, Because TS Doesn't Ship Them Built-In)
Production codebases frequently need DeepPartial/DeepReadonly beyond the shallow built-ins — these are commonly hand-rolled with recursive mapped + conditional types:
type DeepPartial<T> = T extends object
  ? { [K in keyof T]?: DeepPartial<T[K]> }
  : T;
type DeepReadonly<T> = T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;
15.8 Branded / Nominal Types
TypeScript's structural typing means type UserId = string and type OrderId = string are interchangeable — a common source of bugs when IDs of different entities get swapped. Branding simulates nominal typing to prevent this:
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: "UserId" };
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: "OrderId" };
function getUser(id: UserId) { /* ... */ }
function toUserId(id: string): UserId { return id as UserId; } // one controlled cast point
const uid = toUserId("u-123");
// getUser("u-123");   // Incorrect — a raw string is not a UserId
getUser(uid);           // Correct
This pattern is standard in large financial/e-commerce codebases where mixing up an ID type is a real, costly bug class.


  15.9 Required, Readonly, and Combining Utility Types
type CompleteConfig = Required<Readonly<Partial<AppConfig>>>;
Utility types compose freely — chaining them is idiomatic and preferred over hand-writing an equivalent mapped type from scratch (DRY, and it leans on well-tested compiler-provided primitives).
15.10 Production Guidance
- Prefer built-in utility types over custom equivalents whenever one exists — they're battle-tested and instantly recognizable to any TS developer.
- Derive types from a single source of truth (typeof someConstObject,ReturnType<typeof someFn>) rather than maintaining two parallel, potentially-drifting definitions.
- Keep custom deep/recursive utility types in one shared types/utils.tsfile — don't redefineDeepPartialin five different modules.
16. Design Patterns in TypeScript
Design patterns are reusable solutions to recurring design problems. TypeScript's type system (interfaces, generics, access modifiers) makes classic Gang-of-Four patterns considerably safer and more expressive than in plain JavaScript.
16.1 Singleton
Ensures a class has exactly one instance, with a global access point. Use sparingly — it's effectively global mutable state and hinders testability.
class ConfigManager {
  private static instance: ConfigManager;
  private constructor(private readonly settings: Record<string, string>) {}
  static getInstance(): ConfigManager {
    if (!ConfigManager.instance) {
      ConfigManager.instance = new ConfigManager(loadSettingsFromEnv());
    }
    return ConfigManager.instance;
  }
}
Production note: in most Node.js backends, module-level const exports already behave like singletons (a module is only ever evaluated once), making the classic Singleton class often unnecessary boilerplate — prefer a simple exported instance unless you specifically need lazy initialization or subclassing.
16.2 Factory
Encapsulates object-creation logic, decoupling callers from concrete classes.
interface PaymentProcessor { charge(amount: number): Promise<void>; }
class StripeProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { async charge(amount: number) { /* ... */ } }
class PaypalProcessor implements PaymentProcessor { async charge(amount: number) { /* ... */ } }
type ProcessorType = "stripe" | "paypal";
function createPaymentProcessor(type: ProcessorType): PaymentProcessor {
  switch (type) {
    case "stripe": return new StripeProcessor();
    case "paypal": return new PaypalProcessor();
  }
}
16.3 Strategy
Encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind a common interface, selected at runtime.
interface DiscountStrategy { apply(price: number): number; }
class NoDiscount implements DiscountStrategy { apply(price: number) { return price; } }
class PercentageDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
  constructor(private readonly percent: number) {}
  apply(price: number) { return price * (1 - this.percent / 100); }
}
class Checkout {
  constructor(private strategy: DiscountStrategy) {}
  total(price: number) { return this.strategy.apply(price); }
}
16.4 Observer
Defines a one-to-many dependency so that when one object changes state, all dependents are notified — the foundation of event systems, reactive state, and pub/sub.
type Listener<T> = (payload: T) => void;
class EventEmitter<T> {
  private listeners: Listener<T>[] = [];
  subscribe(listener: Listener<T>): () => void {
    this.listeners.push(listener);
    return () => { this.listeners = this.listeners.filter(l => l !== listener); }; // unsubscribe fn
  }
  emit(payload: T): void {
    for (const listener of this.listeners) listener(payload);
  }
}
const onUserCreated = new EventEmitter<{ id: number; name: string }>();
const unsubscribe = onUserCreated.subscribe(user => console.log(`Welcome ${user.name}`));


  16.5 Decorator (Structural Pattern, Distinct From TS's @decorator Syntax)
Adds behavior to an object dynamically without altering other objects of the same class.
interface Coffee { cost(): number; description(): string; }
class SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {
  cost() { return 2; }
  description() { return "Coffee"; }
}
class MilkDecorator implements Coffee {
  constructor(private coffee: Coffee) {}
  cost() { return this.coffee.cost() + 0.5; }
  description() { return `${this.coffee.description()} + Milk`; }
}
const order = new MilkDecorator(new SimpleCoffee());
TypeScript also has native @decorator syntax (Stage-3 ECMAScript decorators, used heavily in Angular/NestJS) for annotating classes/methods declaratively — a related but syntactically distinct concept from the GoF Decorator pattern shown above.
16.6 Repository
Abstracts data-access logic behind a domain-focused interface, decoupling business logic from the persistence mechanism (SQL, NoSQL, in-memory, HTTP).
interface UserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}
class PostgresUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  constructor(private readonly db: DatabaseClient) {}
  async findById(id: string) { return this.db.query<User>("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1", [id]); }
  async save(user: User) { /* ... */ }
  async delete(id: string) { /* ... */ }
}
16.7 Adapter
Converts the interface of one class into another interface clients expect — essential when integrating a third-party library whose API shape doesn't match your domain's contracts.
interface NotificationSender { send(to: string, message: string): Promise<void>; }
class TwilioSdkAdapter implements NotificationSender {
  constructor(private readonly twilioClient: ThirdPartyTwilioClient) {}
  async send(to: string, message: string) {
    await this.twilioClient.messages.create({ to, body: message }); // adapts SDK's own shape
  }
}
16.8 Builder
Constructs complex objects step-by-step, useful when an object has many optional configuration fields.
class RequestBuilder {
  private url = "";
  private method: "GET" | "POST" = "GET";
  private headers: Record<string, string> = {};
  setUrl(url: string): this { this.url = url; return this; }
  setMethod(method: "GET" | "POST"): this { this.method = method; return this; }
  addHeader(key: string, value: string): this { this.headers[key] = value; return this; }
  build() { return { url: this.url, method: this.method, headers: this.headers }; }
}
const req = new RequestBuilder().setUrl("/api/users").setMethod("POST").addHeader("Authorization", "Bearer x").build();
Returning this from each method (a "fluent interface") is what enables the chained call style above.
16.9 When to Reach for a Pattern (and When Not To)
Patterns solve specific recurring forces (variability in algorithm, variability in creation, decoupling layers). Applying a pattern where the underlying force doesn't exist adds indirection without benefit — a violation of YAGNI (§17.7). Senior engineers introduce a pattern when a second/third concrete variant of something already exists or is clearly imminent, not speculatively on day one.
17. SOLID, DRY, KISS, YAGNI — Principles Applied With Real TS Code
17.1 S — Single Responsibility Principle
A class/module should have one, and only one, reason to change.
// Incorrect — Violates SRP — this class has THREE reasons to change:
// business rules, persistence, and email formatting.
class UserService {
  createUser(name: string, email: string) {
    if (!email.includes("@")) throw new Error("Invalid email"); // validation
    const user = { id: Date.now(), name, email };
    fs.writeFileSync("users.json", JSON.stringify(user));         // persistence
    console.log(`<html>Welcome ${name}</html>`);                  // presentation/notification
    return user;
  }
}
// Correct — Each concern is its own collaborator with one reason to change
class UserValidator { validate(email: string) { if (!email.includes("@")) throw new Error("Invalid email"); } }
class UserRepository { save(user: User) { /* persistence only */ } }
class WelcomeEmailSender { send(user: User) { /* email formatting/sending only */ } }
class UserService {
  constructor(
    private validator: UserValidator,
    private repo: UserRepository,
    private mailer: WelcomeEmailSender,
  ) {}
  createUser(name: string, email: string) {
    this.validator.validate(email);
    const user = { id: Date.now(), name, email };
    this.repo.save(user);
    this.mailer.send(user);
    return user;
  }
}
17.2 O — Open/Closed Principle
Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification.
// Incorrect — Every new shape requires editing this function — closed for extension
function area(shape: { type: string; [key: string]: any }): number {
  if (shape.type === "circle") return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
  if (shape.type === "square") return shape.side ** 2;
  throw new Error("Unsupported shape"); // grows forever as shapes are added
}
// Correct — New shapes are added by writing a new class — no existing code is touched
interface Shape { area(): number; }
class Circle implements Shape { constructor(private r: number) {} area() { return Math.PI * this.r ** 2; } }
class Square implements Shape { constructor(private s: number) {} area() { return this.s ** 2; } }
class Triangle implements Shape { constructor(private b: number, private h: number) {} area() { return 0.5 * this.b * this.h; } } // added later, zero edits elsewhere
function totalArea(shapes: Shape[]): number {
  return shapes.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s.area(), 0);
}
17.3 L — Liskov Substitution Principle
Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking correctness.
// Incorrect — Classic LSP violation — Square "is-a" Rectangle geometrically, but not behaviorally
class Rectangle {
  constructor(protected width: number, protected height: number) {}
  setWidth(w: number) { this.width = w; }
  setHeight(h: number) { this.height = h; }
  area() { return this.width * this.height; }
}
class Square extends Rectangle {
  override setWidth(w: number) { this.width = w; this.height = w; } // breaks caller expectations
  override setHeight(h: number) { this.width = h; this.height = h; }
}
function resize(rect: Rectangle) {
  rect.setWidth(5);
  rect.setHeight(10);
  console.assert(rect.area() === 50); // FAILS for Square — silently wrong for a subtype
}
// Correct — Model them as siblings behind a shared contract instead of forcing an is-a relationship
interface Shape2 { area(): number; }
class Rectangle2 implements Shape2 { constructor(private w: number, private h: number) {} area() { return this.w * this.h; } }
class Square2 implements Shape2 { constructor(private s: number) {} area() { return this.s * this.s; } }
17.4 I — Interface Segregation Principle
Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they don't use — prefer many small, focused interfaces over one large one.
// Incorrect — A fat interface forces irrelevant implementations
interface Worker { work(): void; eat(): void; sleep(): void; }
class Robot implements Worker {
  work() { /* ... */ }
  eat() { throw new Error("Robots don't eat"); }  // forced, meaningless implementation
  sleep() { throw new Error("Robots don't sleep"); }
}
// Correct — Segregated interfaces — implement only what's relevant
interface Workable { work(): void; }
interface Eatable { eat(): void; }
interface Sleepable { sleep(): void; }
class Human implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable { work() {} eat() {} sleep() {} }
class Robot2 implements Workable { work() {} }
17.5 D — Dependency Inversion Principle
High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.
// Incorrect — UserService (high-level policy) directly depends on a concrete low-level detail
class SqlDatabase { query(sql: string) { /* ... */ } }
class UserService {
  private db = new SqlDatabase(); // tightly coupled, impossible to swap or mock cleanly
}
// Correct — Both depend on an abstraction; the concrete implementation is INJECTED, not constructed internally
interface Database { query<T>(sql: string, params?: unknown[]): Promise<T[]>; }
class UserService {
  constructor(private readonly db: Database) {} // depends on the interface, not a concrete class
}
class PostgresDatabase implements Database { async query<T>(sql: string) { return [] as T[]; } }
class InMemoryTestDatabase implements Database { async query<T>() { return [] as T[]; } } // trivial to swap in tests
const service = new UserService(new PostgresDatabase()); // composition root wires the real dependency
This is the theoretical foundation behind dependency injection frameworks (NestJS @Injectable, InversifyJS, Angular DI) — DI containers exist purely to automate the "wire up concrete implementations to abstractions" step at the composition root.
17.6 DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself
Every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous, authoritative representation.
// Incorrect — The "shape of a user" is duplicated across three call sites — a change to User must be hunted down everywhere
function createUser(id: number, name: string, email: string) { return { id, name, email }; }
function updateUser(user: { id: number; name: string; email: string }) { /* ... */ }
function logUser(user: { id: number; name: string; email: string }) { /* ... */ }
// Correct — A single named type is the one source of truth
interface User { id: number; name: string; email: string; }
function createUser2(user: Omit<User, "id">): User { return { id: Date.now(), ...user }; }
function updateUser2(user: User) { /* ... */ }
function logUser2(user: User) { /* ... */ }
Caveat — DRY is about knowledge duplication, not code duplication. Two pieces of code that look similar but represent different business rules that happen to coincide today should often stay separate — prematurely merging them ("DRY-ing" too aggressively) creates a false coupling that causes pain the moment the rules diverge. This nuance is what separates senior engineering judgment from mechanical rule-following.
17.7 KISS & YAGNI
- KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid) — prefer the simplest design that correctly solves the actual problem. A generic, configurable, plugin-based abstraction for a feature with exactly one use case is over-engineering.
- YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) — don't build extensibility, generic type parameters, or configuration options for hypothetical future requirements. Add abstraction when a second real use case appears, not speculatively.
// Incorrect — YAGNI violation — a fully generic, pluggable "rule engine" for a single, simple business rule
interface Rule<T> { evaluate(input: T): boolean; }
class RuleEngine<T> {
  private rules: Rule<T>[] = [];
  addRule(rule: Rule<T>) { this.rules.push(rule); }
  evaluateAll(input: T) { return this.rules.every(r => r.evaluate(input)); }
}
// Correct — KISS — a single, simple, direct function until real variability actually appears
function isEligibleForDiscount(user: User): boolean {
  return user.age >= 65 || user.membershipYears >= 5;
}
17.8 Composition Over Inheritance (Recap With SOLID Lens)
Composition (injecting small collaborators, §11.11) is a natural consequence of applying SRP + DIP together: each collaborator has one responsibility, and the composing class depends on abstractions rather than inheriting concrete behavior — this is why "favor composition over inheritance" is often described as "SOLID applied at the object-relationship level," not a separate, unrelated principle.
18. Error Handling Strategies
18.1 Custom Error Hierarchies
Plain Error loses domain meaning. Production systems define a typed hierarchy so callers can branch on kind of failure, not string-matched messages.
abstract class AppError extends Error {
  abstract readonly statusCode: number;
  abstract readonly code: string;
  constructor(message: string, readonly cause?: unknown) {
    super(message);
    this.name = new.target.name;
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype); // fixes `instanceof` across ES5 targets
  }
}
class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  readonly statusCode = 404;
  readonly code = "NOT_FOUND";
}
class ValidationError extends AppError {
  readonly statusCode = 400;
  readonly code = "VALIDATION_ERROR";
  constructor(message: string, readonly fieldErrors: Record<string, string>) { super(message); }
}
class UnauthorizedError extends AppError {
  readonly statusCode = 401;
  readonly code = "UNAUTHORIZED";
}
function handleError(err: unknown) {
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    return { status: err.statusCode, body: { code: err.code, fields: err.fieldErrors } };
  }
  if (err instanceof AppError) {
    return { status: err.statusCode, body: { code: err.code, message: err.message } };
  }
  // Unknown/unexpected error — never leak internals to the client
  console.error("Unhandled error:", err);
  return { status: 500, body: { code: "INTERNAL_ERROR" } };
}


  18.2 The Result/Either Pattern (Errors as Values)
Throwing exceptions is invisible in a function's type signature — callers can't tell from the type alone that a function might throw. For expected, recoverable failures (e.g., "user not found," "invalid input"), many production TS codebases prefer modeling failure explicitly as a return value, using the discriminated-union pattern from §9.3:
type Result<T, E = AppError> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: E };
function parseAge(input: string): Result<number, ValidationError> {
  const n = Number(input);
  if (Number.isNaN(n) || n < 0) {
    return { ok: false, error: new ValidationError("Invalid age", { age: input }) };
  }
  return { ok: true, value: n };
}
const result = parseAge("abc");
if (!result.ok) {
  console.error(result.error.message); // compiler forces you to check `.ok` before accessing `.value`
} else {
  console.log(result.value);
}
Guideline for choosing between throw and Result: reserve throw/exceptions for truly exceptional, unrecoverable, or programmer-error conditions (invariant violations, unreachable code, infrastructure failures). Use Result for expected business-level failure paths that the caller is meant to explicitly handle — it makes failure part of the type contract instead of a hidden control-flow path.
18.3 Never Swallow Errors Silently
// Incorrect — Silently swallowed — the failure vanishes, debugging becomes forensics
try { await sendEmail(user); } catch {}
// Correct — At minimum, log with context; usually also re-throw or return a Result
try {
  await sendEmail(user);
} catch (error) {
  logger.error("Failed to send welcome email", { userId: user.id, error });
  throw new AppError("Email delivery failed", { cause: error });
}
18.4 Runtime Validation at Trust Boundaries (Critical, Often-Missed Practice)
TypeScript types are erased at runtime and provide zero protection against malformed data arriving from outside your program — HTTP request bodies, JSON.parse results, environment variables, third-party API responses, message-queue payloads. Casting them with as SomeType is a lie the compiler will happily believe. Production-grade code validates untyped input with a runtime schema library (Zod, Valibot, io-ts) at every trust boundary, and derives the static type from the schema (single source of truth — DRY):
import { z } from "zod";
const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.number(),
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email(),
});
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>; // static type derived FROM the runtime schema
async function getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const json: unknown = await res.json();
  return UserSchema.parse(json); // throws a descriptive ZodError if the shape is wrong — validated at the boundary
}
Environment variables deserve the same treatment — never sprinkle raw process.env.SOME_VAR! (non-null assertion) throughout a codebase; validate them once, at startup:
const EnvSchema = z.object({
  DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  PORT: z.coerce.number().default(3000),
  NODE_ENV: z.enum(["development", "production", "test"]),
});
export const env = EnvSchema.parse(process.env); // crashes fast at boot if misconfigured, not mid-request
18.5 Fail Fast, Fail Loud
Prefer guard clauses at the top of a function over deeply nested conditionals, and prefer crashing on startup for unrecoverable configuration errors over limping along with undefined values that surface as confusing failures three layers deeper.
19. Testing TypeScript
19.1 The Testing Pyramid, Applied to TS Projects
Most test value-per-dollar comes from a large base of fast unit tests plus a thinner layer of integration tests around real I/O boundaries, with only a handful of E2E tests protecting critical user journeys.
19.2 Unit Testing With Jest/Vitest
// discount.ts
export function applyDiscount(price: number, percent: number): number {
  if (percent < 0 || percent > 100) throw new RangeError("percent must be 0-100");
  return price * (1 - percent / 100);
}
// discount.test.ts
import { describe, it, expect } from "vitest"; // or from "@jest/globals"
import { applyDiscount } from "./discount";
describe("applyDiscount", () => {
  it("applies a percentage discount correctly", () => {
    expect(applyDiscount(100, 20)).toBe(80);
  });
  it("throws for out-of-range percentages", () => {
    expect(() => applyDiscount(100, 150)).toThrow(RangeError);
  });
});
19.3 Testability Through Dependency Injection
Because §17.5 (DIP) means collaborators are injected via constructor rather than hard-coded, they can be trivially substituted with test doubles:
class FakeClock { now(): Date { return new Date("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"); } }
class SubscriptionService {
  constructor(private clock: { now(): Date }) {}
  isExpired(expiresAt: Date): boolean { return this.clock.now() > expiresAt; }
}
// test
const service = new SubscriptionService(new FakeClock());
expect(service.isExpired(new Date("2025-01-01"))).toBe(true);
Classes that construct their own dependencies internally (new SqlDatabase() inside the class body) cannot be unit tested without hitting real infrastructure — this is the single most common reason production codebases retrofit dependency injection after the fact.
19.4 Mocking With Types Preserved
import { vi } from "vitest";
interface EmailClient { send(to: string, body: string): Promise<void>; }
const mockEmailClient: EmailClient = { send: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(undefined) };
// mockEmailClient still satisfies the EmailClient interface — full type safety in the test too
19.5 Type-Level Testing
Beyond runtime behavior, you can assert that your types behave correctly — critical for generic utility types and public library APIs:
import { expectTypeOf } from "vitest";
expectTypeOf(applyDiscount(100, 10)).toBeNumber();
expectTypeOf<Pick<User, "id">>().toEqualTypeOf<{ id: number }>();
Libraries like tsd or Vitest's built-in expectTypeOf catch regressions where a refactor accidentally widens/narrows a public type signature — a class of bug that ordinary runtime tests cannot detect at all.
19.6 Testing Discriminated Unions & Exhaustiveness
function describeStatus(s: Status): string {
  switch (s.status) {
    case "success": return "OK";
    case "error": return "Failed";
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = s; // if a new Status variant is added without updating this switch,
      throw new Error(`Unhandled: ${_exhaustive}`); // this LINE fails to compile — caught before tests even run
    }
  }
}
19.7 Integration Testing Boundaries
// Use a real (but ephemeral/containerized) database for repository-layer tests,
// rather than mocking the database driver — mocking your own persistence layer
// only tests that your mocks behave as you told them to, not real SQL behavior.
describe("PostgresUserRepository (integration)", () => {
  it("persists and retrieves a user", async () => {
    const repo = new PostgresUserRepository(testDbClient);
    await repo.save({ id: "1", name: "Test User", email: "t@example.com" });
    const found = await repo.findById("1");
    expect(found?.name).toBe("Test User");
  });
});
19.8 Coverage & Test-Quality Guidance
- Coverage percentage is a lagging proxy metric, not a goal — 100% coverage with no meaningful assertions is worthless. Focus on covering business rules and edge cases (empty arrays, boundary numbers, error paths), not on the percentage number itself.
- Test behavior/contracts, not implementation details — a good unit test should survive an internal refactor that doesn't change observable behavior; a test that breaks on every refactor is over-coupled to implementation.
- Prefer real objects over mocks when the collaborator is cheap and deterministic (e.g., a pure DiscountCalculator); reserve mocks for genuinely expensive/non-deterministic boundaries (network, time, randomness, filesystem).
20. Tooling, Linting, Build Systems & CI/CD for Production TS
20.1 Linting & Formatting
npm install -D eslint @typescript-eslint/parser @typescript-eslint/eslint-plugin prettier eslint-config-prettier
Key rules production teams enable beyond the defaults:
| Rule | Purpose | 
|---|---|
| @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any | Bans any— forcesunknown+ narrowing instead | 
| @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises | Catches un-awaited/un-caught Promises — a top source of silent async bugs | 
| @typescript-eslint/no-non-null-assertion | Flags !usage for review | 
| @typescript-eslint/explicit-function-return-type | Requires explicit return types on exported functions | 
| no-var | Bans legacy var | 
| eqeqeq | Requires ===/!==over==/!= | 
Run Prettier for formatting (line length, quotes, spacing) and ESLint purely for correctness/style rules — don't let ESLint fight Prettier over formatting; eslint-config-prettier disables the overlapping rules.
20.2 Pre-Commit Enforcement
npm install -D husky lint-staged
npx husky init
// package.json
"lint-staged": {
  "*.{ts,tsx}": ["eslint --fix", "prettier --write"]
}
Catching issues at commit time (rather than in CI, minutes later) shortens the feedback loop dramatically.
20.3 Build Tooling Choices
| Tool | Role | When to Use | 
|---|---|---|
| tsc | Type-checking (always) + emit (optionally) | Libraries; type-check gate in CI for any project | 
| esbuild/swc | Extremely fast transpilation (types stripped, NOT checked) | App bundling where tsc --noEmitruns separately as the type gate | 
| Vite | Dev server + bundler for frontend apps (uses esbuild/swc under the hood) | Frontend SPAs | 
| tsup | Zero-config bundler for TS libraries (wraps esbuild) | Publishing npm packages | 
| ts-node/tsx | Run .tsfiles directly without a separate compile step | Scripts, local dev, quick iteration | 
Critical production pattern: bundlers that use esbuild/swc transpile TypeScript (strip types) but do not type-check it — a type error can ship to production silently if tsc isn't run as a separate step. Every production CI pipeline must run tsc --noEmit as an explicit gate, independent of the bundler.
20.4 CI Pipeline (Representative)
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
name: CI
on: [push, pull_request]
jobs:
  verify:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 20, cache: "npm" }
      - run: npm ci                    # reproducible install from package-lock.json
      - run: npm run typecheck          # tsc --noEmit
      - run: npm run lint               # eslint .
      - run: npm run test -- --coverage
      - run: npm run build
Order matters and is deliberate: typecheck → lint → test → build. Fail fast on the cheapest checks first (typecheck/lint are seconds; full test suites and builds are much slower) so a broken PR gets feedback as quickly as possible.
20.5 Monorepo Considerations
For multi-package repos (npm/pnpm/Turborepo/Nx workspaces), use TypeScript Project References (composite: true per package + references pointing to dependency packages) so tsc --build incrementally recompiles only what changed, and each package gets its own .d.ts output consumable by sibling packages without a full rebuild.
20.6 Semantic Versioning & Public API Discipline
For published libraries, .d.ts output is your public contract. Treat any change to an exported type as a potential breaking change subject to semver rules — adding a new required property to an exported interface is a major version bump even though it might "just be a type change," because it can break every consumer's compilation.
21. Performance, Compiler Internals & Scaling Large Codebases
21.1 Compile-Time Performance
- 
skipLibCheck: true— skips re-checking every dependency's.d.tsfiles; a large compile-time win with negligible safety cost in practice (dependencies are assumed to type-check themselves before publishing).
- 
Project References + incremental: true— enables.tsbuildinfocaching so unchanged files aren't rechecked on every build.
- 
Avoid deeply recursive/huge union/conditional types in hot paths of your type definitions — the compiler has real algorithmic limits (recursion depth caps, instantiation limits) and pathological generic code can measurably slow down tscand editor responsiveness across a whole team.
- 
Prefer interfaceover largetypeintersections for object shapes that are checked often — interfaces are cached/named internally in a way that tends to check faster than resolving deep intersection chains repeatedly.
21.2 Runtime Performance (What TS Does and Doesn't Affect)
Types are erased — TypeScript itself adds zero runtime overhead. Runtime performance is purely a function of the emitted JavaScript and your algorithms/data structures, same as any JS codebase:
- Choose the right target(§2.3) — targeting too old an ES version forces the compiler to down-level modern syntax (e.g., generators, classes) into more verbose, slower polyfilled code; target the newest runtime you actually support (modern Node LTS, or your supported browser matrix).
- Enums (§8.1) that are not const enumgenerate an extra runtime object — negligible for most apps, but worth knowing at scale (thousands of enum members across a bundle).
- Excess use of getters/setters or Proxy-based reactivity (common in some state-management libraries) has real runtime cost per access — profile before assuming it's free.
21.3 Scaling Type-Checking Across Large Codebases
- Split large monorepos into TypeScript project references so IDE responsiveness and tsc --buildscale with changed code, not total code.
- Avoid barrel files (§13.3) that re-export hundreds of modules from one entry point in large packages — they force the compiler (and bundlers) to load far more than is needed for a single import.
- Periodically audit for anyleakage using@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-anyin CI —anyis contagious (anything derived from ananybecomesany), and left unchecked it silently erodes type coverage across a growing codebase.
21.4 Architectural Guidance for Large Systems
- 
Composition root pattern — construct the full dependency graph (concrete implementations wired to abstractions, §17.5) in exactly one place (e.g., main.ts/bootstrap.ts), keeping every other module ignorant of concrete implementations.
- Bounded contexts / feature modules (§13.7) — keep domain boundaries explicit; cross-feature imports should go through a clearly exported public surface, not reach into another feature's internals.
- Anti-corruption layer — when integrating an external system (legacy API, third-party SDK) whose data model doesn't match your domain, wrap it behind an Adapter (§16.7) so external model changes don't ripple through your core domain types.
- 
Immutable-by-default data flow — prefer readonly,ReadonlyArray<T>, and returning new objects over mutation; it drastically reduces a whole class of "who mutated this and when" bugs in large teams, at the cost of being slightly more verbose.
22. Interview Cheat Sheet (Expanded)
22.1 Frequently Asked Interview Questions
- What is TypeScript and how does it differ from JavaScript?
- What are the benefits of using TypeScript in a production codebase?
- Explain anyvsunknownvsnevervsvoid.
- What's the difference between interfaceandtype? When would you choose one over the other?
- What are Enums, and why might you prefer a const-object + union-literal pattern instead?
- What is a type assertion (as), and how does it differ from a type guard?
- What are generics? Give a real example beyond identity<T>.
- What is the difference between keyofandtypeofas type operators?
- How does async/awaitrelate to Promises and the event loop?
- What is a discriminated union, and how does exhaustiveness checking work?
- Explain structural typing vs nominal typing, and how "branded types" simulate nominal typing in TS.
- What is the Dependency Inversion Principle, and how does it show up in TypeScript code?
- Why is strictNullChecksimportant, and what class of bugs does it eliminate?
- What happens to TypeScript types at runtime?
- How would you validate an untrusted API response in a type-safe way?
22.2 Common Mistakes (What Senior Reviewers Flag in PRs)
- Using anyas a default escape hatch instead ofunknown+ narrowing.
- Not enabling strictmode (or worse, disabling individual strict flags to "make errors go away").
- Ignoring null/undefinedhandling instead of modeling absence explicitly.
- Overusing astype assertions instead of writing/using a proper type guard.
- Mutating data in place instead of using readonly/immutable patterns.
- Reaching for classes/interfaces for every object instead of plain types where appropriate — over-engineering simple data shapes.
- Overusing the non-null assertion !to silence the compiler instead of handling the null case.
- Forgetting to handle errors in asynccode (missingtry/catch, or an un-awaited Promise that produces anunhandledRejection).
- Deep inheritance chains where composition + interfaces would be more flexible and testable.
- Casting untrusted external JSON directly to a type without runtime validation (§18.4).
22.3 Best Practices Checklist
- Enable strict: true(and ideallynoUncheckedIndexedAccess,exactOptionalPropertyTypes) intsconfig.json.
- Prefer interfacefor object/class shapes;typefor unions, tuples, and mapped/conditional types.
- Use unknown(neverany) at true type boundaries, narrowed via guards or a schema validator.
- Prefer readonlyfor data that shouldn't be mutated by the receiver.
- Let TypeScript infer local variable types; explicitly annotate exported function signatures.
- Handle errors explicitly in async code; never swallow exceptions silently.
- Keep functions and classes small and single-purpose (SRP).
- Depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concrete implementations (DIP) — inject collaborators.
- Validate all external/untrusted data at runtime, not just at the type level.
22.4 Naming Conventions
| Element | Convention | Example | 
|---|---|---|
| Variables | camelCase | userName | 
| Functions | camelCase | getUserData() | 
| Classes / Interfaces | PascalCase | UserService,UserRepository | 
| Types / Enums | PascalCase | UserType,StatusEnum | 
| Constants (true, global constants) | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | MAX_LIMIT | 
| Generic type parameters | Single uppercase letter, or T-prefixed for clarity in complex code | T,K,TEntity | 
| Files | kebab-case | user-service.ts | 
| Folders | kebab-case | user-service/ | 
Consistent naming — enforced by a shared ESLint config, not tribal knowledge — is what keeps a codebase readable as team size grows.
22.5 Recommended Production Folder Structure (Recap)
src/
├── assets/          # images, icons, fonts
├── modules/         # feature-based folders: controller/service/repository/types per feature
├── shared/
│   ├── types/       # cross-cutting shared types
│   ├── utils/       # pure helper functions
│   └── errors/      # AppError hierarchy
├── config/          # validated environment/config (Zod-parsed at boot)
└── index.ts         # composition root / entry point
Why this structure: separates concerns clearly, scales cleanly as features are added/removed, improves reusability of shared code, and makes team collaboration easier since features are self-contained.
23. One-Page Quick Revision Sheet
23.1 Important Syntax
let name: string = "Mahesh";
const PI: number = 3.14;
function add(a: number, b: number): number { return a + b; }
const add2 = (a: number, b: number): number => a + b;
interface User { id: number; name: string; }
type ID = number | string;
class User2 { constructor(public name: string) {} }
enum Status { Active, Inactive }
const nums: number[] = [1, 2, 3];
const user: [number, string] = [1, "Mahesh"];
let data: any;
let value: unknown;
let len = (value as string).length;
23.2 Most Used Keywords
let · const · function · return · if / else · for · while · switch · break · continue · class · interface · type · enum · extends · implements · public / private / protected / readonly · static · async · await · try / catch / finally · throw · import / export / from · keyof · typeof · infer · satisfies · as
23.3 Learning Roadmap Recap
1. Basics → 2. OOP Concepts → 3. Advanced Types → 4. Modules → 5. Async JS → 6. Patterns/SOLID → 7. Testing/Tooling → 8. Build & Ship
| Stage | Covers | 
|---|---|
| 1. Basics | Variables, primitive types, functions, arrays, tuples | 
| 2. OOP Concepts | Classes, interfaces, inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation | 
| 3. Advanced Types | Generics, utility types, conditional/mapped types | 
| 4. Modules | Import/export, project structure, path aliases | 
| 5. Async JS | Promises, async/await, error handling, combinators | 
| 6. Patterns/SOLID | GoF patterns, SOLID, DRY/KISS/YAGNI | 
| 7. Testing/Tooling | Unit/integration tests, ESLint, CI pipelines | 
| 8. Build & Ship | Compiler config, bundlers, performance, monorepo scaling | 
23.4 One-Page Summary
- TypeScript = JavaScript + a static structural type system, fully erased at compile time.
- It catches errors early, documents intent, and enables safe large-scale refactoring.
- 
unknown+ narrowing is the type-safe replacement forany.
- Discriminated unions + exhaustiveness checks are the idiomatic way to model variant state.
- SOLID principles map directly onto everyday TS decisions: small classes (S), extend-don't-edit (O), honest subtyping (L), focused interfaces (I), inject abstractions (D).
- Types provide zero runtime protection — validate all external data with a schema library at trust boundaries.
- Prefer composition, dependency injection, and immutability by default for testable, maintainable, large-scale systems.
- Type-check (tsc --noEmit) as an explicit, separate CI step — bundlers like esbuild/swc do not type-check.
23.5 Final Notes
Consistency + practice + real projects = mastery. Build small projects deliberately using strict mode, layered architecture, dependency injection, and tests from day one — the habits formed on a small project are exactly the habits that scale (or fail to scale) on a large one.
